WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
This is hell.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Somebody has gone to jail in the wake of the U.S. torture program that was instituted and maintained during the Bush administration.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Unfortunately, the only person to go to jail for it isn't one of the torturers themselves.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
It isn't their commanding officer who ordered the torture.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.000
It's not the person who came up with the torture policy, and it's definitely not anyone in the upper reaches of the Bush administration who endorsed and supported torture.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Nope, it's the person who tried to stop torture by revealing it to the public that waterboarding was taking place.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:41.000
And despite already serving time, the government still isn't done with persecuting our first guest here to tell us about his case as well as other whistleblowers

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.000
and what all this means for the future of the U.S. post-election day.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:52.000
John Kiriakou is co-chair with past guest Tom Drake of the Whistleblowers Public Exchange Campaign.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.000
You can find out more about that at johnkiriakou.com. Welcome to This is Hell, John.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Hey, thanks very much for having me.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.000
It's great having you on our show. It's really an honor. Thank you so much for being on our show.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
John is an associate fellow with Institute for Policy Studies.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.000
One of our correspondents, Laura Carlson, our correspondent in Mexico City, is also a member of that organization.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:20.000
John was a CIA analyst, case officer, senior investigator for the Senate Foreign Relations Committee and counterterrorism consultant for ABC News.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.000
John, you were recently given the Sam Adams Award for integrity and intelligence.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.000
An award named after Samuel A. Adams, a CIA whistleblower during the Vietnam War.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.000
And it's an award that's given out by a group of retired CIA officers, many who have appeared here on This is Hell.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Adams blew the whistle on the CIA in military purposely under-reporting North Vietnamese and Viet Cong troop levels.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:51.000
He believed this was being done to give the impression the enemy was weaker than it was and thus get political approval for continuing the war.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Adams also testified in the Pentagon Papers case in defense of past This is Hell guest Dan Ellsberg.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Before we get to your case and why you were given the Sam Adams Award, while I'm certain it varies from situation to situation depending on where it occurs or within what organization,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.000
how much has whistleblowing changed since the Pentagon Papers Vietnam era?

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Is it now easier or more difficult to be a whistleblower?

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.000
You know what, I think it's just as difficult now as it was for Dan Ellsberg back in 1970.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:28.000
And it's because there's really no mechanism, especially if you're a potential whistleblower in the national security realm.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.000
There's just no mechanism to blow the whistle.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Look at my situation, for example, everybody's taught to go through the chain of command, right?

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Tom Drake went through the chain of command at NSA and it bought him nine felony charges.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.000
My chain of command created the torture program.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.000
So the only place for me to go with this information was the press.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.000
And of course that bought me five felony counts, including three counts of espionage.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.000
So really it hasn't changed since Dan Ellsberg.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:58.000
He was charged with espionage by the Nixon administration.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:05.000
So when we hear people who are critical of the way in which the Obama administration has gone after whistleblowers, how fair is that?

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Because has it changed much?

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:15.000
How would you rate Obama's record on whistleblowers and how much it has changed since George W. Bush, Bill Clinton, George H.W. Bush, Reagan?

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Is this a consistent policy against whistleblowers or has it actually changed during Obama?

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.000
It's not. It's accelerated under the Obama administration.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:32.000
Between the original passage of the Espionage Act in 1917 and Barack Obama's inauguration in 2009,

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:40.000
three Americans were charged with espionage for quote-unquote leaking classified information to the press.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:46.000
One under Nixon, one under Reagan, and one under Clinton.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:58.000
But since Obama became president, he has charged nine people with espionage, all of which were accused, or all of whom I should say were accused of leaking information to the press.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.000
So it's really accelerated under Obama.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:10.000
And I've said this in writing. I've said that Obama is even more Nixonian than Nixon was in his obsession with leaks.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:26.000
So what you leaked to the press, could you go back in history and find cases in which people leaked even possibly more dangerous information to the press that was not prosecuted in the way that you were prosecuted?

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Oh, oh, listen, we don't even have to go back very far. Let's look at General David Petraeus, the former CIA director.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:42.000
You know, I was charged and convicted of confirming the name of a former CIA colleague to a reporter who never published the name.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:49.000
And I was facing 45 years in prison. I ended up taking a plea and being sentenced to two and a half years in prison.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:05:05.000
David Petraeus leaked the names of 10 covert CIA operatives to his adulterous girlfriend, who he was trying to impress, as well as having given her access to what are called the presidential black books.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:12.000
These are the most highly classified documents that the CIA has created, the most highly classified documents that exist anywhere in government.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:27.000
But he was charged with a misdemeanor. He got 18 months of unsupervised probation, a fine, and at his sentencing, the judge came down from the bench to shake his hand and thank him for his service to the country.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:33.000
So to you, what explains the difference in treatment?

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:51.000
You know, I've always blamed it on Eric Holder. I think it was really Eric Holder who had this incredible obsession with secrecy, especially secrecy of issues and operations that had nothing to do with the Obama administration, but were holdovers from the Bush administration.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:06:11.000
And I think that Holder successfully argued to Obama that this was something that needed to be done for the bigger picture so that potential whistleblowers saw the price that whistleblowers were paying and would decide to keep their mouths shut.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.000
I think that's really what the end game was.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:27.000
When you were giving the same Adams Award, the CIA retirees who gave the award to you noted in 2002, quote, you led the team that located Abu Zubeda alleged to be a high ranking member of Al Qaeda.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:42.000
It later transpired that Zubeda was waterboarded 83 times. You were the first U.S. government official to confirm during a national news interview in December 2007 that waterboarding, which you described as torture, was used to interrogate all Al Qaeda prisoners.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:53.000
Kariakau subsequently faced persecution by the U.S. government for his act of truth telling and was sentenced to a 30 month prison term ostensibly for revealing classified information.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:07:01.000
To this day, Kariakau remains the sole U.S. government official past or present who has gone to jail over the issue of torture in the post-911 era.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:10.000
Your claims of U.S. torture practices were later confirmed by President Obama, who in 2014 publicly acknowledged that we tortured some folks.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:29.000
As The New York Times reported back in January 2013, and as you were saying, you were given this 30 month sentence as part of a plea deal in which you admitted violating the Intelligence Identities Protection Act by emailing the name of a covert CIA officer to a freelance reporter who, as you were pointing out earlier, did not publish it.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:34.000
Why did you believe it was necessary to reveal a CIA operative's identity to a reporter?

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:42.000
Well, it wasn't necessary and it was a mistake. It was a momentary lapse in judgment. But let me explain how it all happened.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:52.000
This reporter, this freelance reporter who later went to ABC News, was writing a book on the CIA's rendition detention and interrogation program.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:08:00.000
And he asked me if I could introduce him to anybody who had been involved in renditions, which is just a fancy word for kidnapping.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.000
And I said, no, I've kidnapping wasn't my thing. I really didn't know any of those people when I was working at the CIA.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:11.000
So he sent me a list of a dozen names. And he said, can you introduce me to any of these people?

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:15.000
I said, no. I looked at the names. I said, I don't have any idea who any of these people are.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.000
About a week later, he sent me another list of another dozen names.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:25.000
And I said, I don't know any of these people. You obviously know this issue so much better than I. I really can't help you.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:33.000
And he said, well, what about the guy that you mentioned in your book? You call him, I'll say, John, can you introduce me to him?

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:40.000
And I said, oh, you're talking about John Doe. I think he's probably retired and living in Virginia somewhere. I don't have any idea.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:46.000
That was the felony. That was the crime. And they came down on me like a ton of bricks for it.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:54.000
Wow. So when you were saying those words, when you were communicating that message to him, did you immediately realize that you had just found him?

00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:00.000
Did you immediately realize that you had just violated the Intelligence Identities Protection Act or did you feel that it didn't fall underneath that law at the time?

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:13.000
No, it never even occurred to me because the Intelligence Identities Protection Act says that the officer being referenced has to either be overseas undercover or have been overseas undercover in the previous five years.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:21.000
I just assumed he had been retired for a decade. I had no idea that he had been overseas, let alone overseas undercover.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:34.000
But it didn't matter. My judge said in my very first hearing that she was not going to respect legal precedent that I had to have criminal intent when committing the crime.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:41.000
And my attorney stood up and said, Your Honor, are you saying that a person can accidentally commit espionage and then be charged and tried for it?

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:47.000
And she said, that's exactly what I'm saying. So I knew in that very first hearing that I didn't have a prayer.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:55.000
If I had elected to go to trial, I knew that I was going to have to really fight at the appeals level because I was going to lose at the district level.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:00.000
You know, we are recently speaking to an attorney who has represented some of the Gitmo detainees.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:07.420
And he was talking about this concept that he has, this idea that he has, that the war

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:13.280
on terror has now not only, you know, seeped throughout the national security apparatus

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:17.440
of the United States, but it has gotten into the Justice Department.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:24.160
It's gotten into the way in which we execute justice here in the United States today.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:28.840
Do you think that the war on terror, this permanent war footing, has now had an effect

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:33.720
on the way in which our criminal justice system works and the way our court system works?

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:38.440
Oh, I do. Very, very much. I agree with that strongly. And I'll explain why.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:43.080
The Justice Department has developed two techniques that they use, and this really started

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:47.560
in the immediate aftermath of 9-11. And now we see it everywhere in the court system,

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:52.600
at least at the federal level. The feds do two things. One is called venue shopping,

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:58.360
where they look to charge you in the district, the federal district,

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:03.000
where they believe they are most likely to get a conviction and where they believe you

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.920
will get the longest sentence. For example, almost all national security cases are charged

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:14.120
in the Eastern District of Virginia, which is based in Alexandria, Virginia. No national

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:20.040
security defendant has ever won a case in the Eastern District of Virginia. I was charged there,

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:25.160
Jeffrey Sterling was charged there, and Ed Snowden's case is being charged in the

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:29.800
Eastern District of Virginia. Now that doesn't really make any sense, because when Snowden made

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:35.240
his revelations, he was officially a resident of Hawaii, which is the Northern District of

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:40.920
California. And NSA, for which he was working, is located in Fort Meade, Maryland, which

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:45.560
is the federal district of Maryland. So Maryland and Northern California,

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:51.160
but Snowden is charged in the Eastern District of Virginia, because the same judge that Jeffrey

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:56.520
Sterling and I had, Judge Leanne Brinkama, reserves all national security cases for herself,

00:11:56.520 --> 00:12:03.400
and she's never acquitted anybody. So that's one thing. The other thing is called charge stacking.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:09.320
Let's say that you actually have done something, and I'll use my case. I really did confirm the

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:17.240
name of that former colleague. So I probably was guilty, well, I didn't have criminal intent,

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:21.160
but I technically was guilty of violating the Intelligence Identities Protection Act.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:27.400
But the government charged me with five felonies, four, I'm sorry, three counts of espionage and

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:31.560
one count of making a false statement. And I was never really sure what the false statement was

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:35.800
supposed to have been. But anyway, I had to defend myself against five felonies. So what

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:43.160
they do is they'll drag this out for a year until you run up a million plus in legal fees,

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:48.840
and then they come back and they say, okay, tell you what, we'll drop four of these five charges

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:54.840
if you take a guilty plea to the one charge. Well, the government wins 98.2% of its cases,

00:12:54.840 --> 00:13:01.800
according to ProPublica. And my attorneys told me, we can go to trial and throw the dice,

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:08.840
you have a 1.8% chance of winning, and it's going to cost you another two to three million.

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:16.280
So what do you want to do? If you're convicted, you're realistically facing 18 to 24 years,

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:20.200
or do you want to take their 30 months and you'll be home in 23? So it becomes really an

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:25.320
economic decision. And this is new. This is why the government wins so many of its cases,

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:30.840
because the deck is stacked. Why would juries find the government,

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:35.560
you know, side with the government in so many of these cases? What explains to you

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:40.920
juries agreeing with the government 98% of the time? Well, they don't necessarily.

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:49.720
95% of that 98.2% are the results of plea bargains. And the plea bargains are the result of charge

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:54.840
stacking. But just to be on the safe side from the government perspective, they charge

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:59.000
these national security cases in the Eastern District of Virginia, because they know that

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:04.200
in the unlikely event that the case goes to trial, as it did with Jeffrey Sterling,

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:10.200
that the jury is going to be made up of people who have friends or family working at the CIA,

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:16.920
the FBI, DOD, the intelligence community contractors. You just don't stand a chance.

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:24.520
You know, you write that in June 2013 in an open letter that you gave Edward Snowden some advice,

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:30.040
and you repeat it, you write, FBI agents will like, trick and deceive you. They will twist

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:35.160
your words and play on your patriotism to entrap you. They'll pretend to be people they are not,

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:41.880
supporters, well-wishers, and friends, all the while wearing wires to record your out-of-contact

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:46.280
statements to use against you. The FBI is the enemy. It's part of the problem, not the

00:14:46.280 --> 00:14:53.000
solution, beware. In your opinion, can reform save the FBI or does it need to be done away

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:57.640
with completely and replaced with a completely different agency? Yeah, I actually like the

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:03.560
British MI5 model where they've got this domestic investigatory organization, but it does not have

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:10.040
arrest powers. Let me tell you something else about the FBI. This happened to me when I was

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:17.800
still working on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee staff. It was early 2011. As part of

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:23.160
that job, we used to have lunch with foreign diplomats all the time, two, three, four times a

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:29.480
week. And you talk about the events of the day, exchange information, opinions on developments.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:34.440
In my case, it was in the Middle East. Then I got a call one day from a Japanese diplomat who wanted

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:38.840
to have lunch. And I remember the lunch very distinctly. We talked about Israeli elections

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:43.800
and Turkish elections, and we talked about the Arab-Israeli peace process. And at the end of

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:48.840
the lunch, this Japanese diplomat says to me, so what's next for you? And I said, well,

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:53.240
I think I'm going to resign soon. I promised Senator Kerry that I would give him two years.

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:56.520
It's been two and a half, and I'd like to go back into the private sector.

00:15:57.560 --> 00:16:03.560
And very excitedly, he said, no, no, don't do that. If you give me information, I can give you

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:09.720
money. And I said, you should be ashamed of yourself or of yourself. You know how many

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:16.120
times I've made that pitch? So I ended the lunch and I went directly to the office of the

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:21.640
Senate Security Officer. And I said, I was just pitched by a foreign intelligence officer.

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:26.680
So he asked me to write it up as a memo. He sent it to the FBI. Two days later,

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:31.480
two FBI agents came to interview me. And they said, here's what we want you to do.

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:37.480
Call him back, invite him to lunch, and try to get him to tell you exactly what information he's

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:42.200
looking for and how much he's willing to pay for it. And I said, okay, you want me to wear

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:46.840
a wire? They said, no, we're going to be at the next table. I said, okay. Well, the day of the

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:50.440
lunch, they called back to say that something had come up and they weren't going to be at the next

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:54.440
table. But just to go ahead with the lunch, write it up as a memo and send it to them.

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:59.400
So I did that. And they asked me to do it a third time and a fourth time and a fifth time,

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:05.080
which I did. And so finally, this diplomat told me that he had been promoted. He had been

00:17:05.080 --> 00:17:10.280
transferred to Cairo. It was his dream job. I shook his hand. Best of luck. Never saw him again.

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:17.320
A year later, after my arrest, we received discovery from the Justice Department. And it turned out that

00:17:17.320 --> 00:17:24.200
there never was any Japanese diplomat. He was an FBI agent pretending to be a Japanese diplomat

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:31.000
and trying to get me to commit actual espionage. But I kept foiling them because I kept writing

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:36.280
these memos. And the memos were utterly comprehensive. So when we saw this, I said

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:42.440
to my lead attorney, why would they do something like this? And he says, because they have no

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:47.720
case against you and they know they have no case against you. You have it committed espionage.

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:54.280
So they were trying to force you to commit real espionage. So that being fresh in my mind is

00:17:54.280 --> 00:18:01.400
what I was warning Ed Snowden against. You can't trust anybody. As recently as three weeks ago,

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:06.920
I got a call from a guy who claimed that he and I were colleagues overseas about 20 years ago.

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:12.840
The name just did not ring a bell to me. But he used a couple names of people that I had been

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:20.920
friendly with. And he said he no longer works for the agency and he wants to do some research.

00:18:20.920 --> 00:18:25.560
He wants to hire me to do his research because he's living overseas now. The story just didn't

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:30.920
make any sense to me. And he kept making mistakes with some of the CIA lingo, which made me

00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:36.680
think that the guy's not a CIA officer. And I don't remember him. But he mentioned an FBI agent

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:45.880
that I used to work with overseas in this post that I can't say where. So I called her.

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:51.640
And much to my surprise, she actually called me back. And I said, hey, I heard from, I'm

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:55.880
going to say again, John Smith. And she says, which John Smith? I said, the one that we

00:18:55.880 --> 00:19:00.280
used to work with overseas. And she goes, Oh yeah, yeah, you're talking about the FBI agent.

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:05.080
And I said, Yeah, the FBI agent. Yeah, how's he doing? I said, Oh, he's okay. He's overseas.

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:11.080
He just called me. I just wanted to tell you that he says hello. So it turns out here they are,

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:16.840
after I've done my time, I've finished my sentence, still trying to entrap me. The guy

00:19:16.840 --> 00:19:23.880
offered me $5,000 a month to do classified research. I don't have a security clearance

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:29.320
anymore, obviously. So he wanted me to go back to my CIA colleagues to try to draw classified

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:34.840
information out of them so that they could charge me with espionage again. So this is something that

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:39.160
not just I have to deal with, this is something that the likes of Tom Drake has to deal with,

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:43.480
and Kirk Wiebe, and Bill Binney, and Jeffrey Sterling, and Chelsea Manning.

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:48.840
Even after you're convicted, even after your case is over, they still come after you.

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:55.640
That is really remarkable. But you had as a CIA operative in the past,

00:19:55.640 --> 00:19:59.160
you had worked with the FBI on many occasions, in fact.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:04.400
When they called you up one day and they said that they want to discuss an investigation

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:08.540
with you, you immediately went down, you went and helped them out, and what they were doing

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:12.440
while they were talking to you, you figured out that you were the target of the investigation

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:17.080
because they told you that they were using a search warrant back on your home.

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:22.160
So the crazy thing is when they called me and asked me to come down to the Washington

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:27.920
Field Office, my exact words were, anything for the FBI, they said, we're working

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:31.520
a case and we need your help, and I said anything for the FBI.

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:33.360
I didn't bother to call my lawyer.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:38.480
I went straight to the Washington Field Office, and it was an hour and 20 minutes into this

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:45.800
interview before I realized that this investigation was against me.

00:20:45.800 --> 00:20:49.840
And when I said, wait a minute, wait a minute, you guys think that I have been leaking

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:54.200
information to Al Qaeda, which is what the accusation was.

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:59.240
And they said, well, we should tell you that as we're speaking, we're executing a search

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:01.440
warrant on your home.

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:07.120
And sure enough, they had sent a dozen FBI agents to my house and they were just ransacking

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:08.120
the place.

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:14.680
How much did your experience then, when this occurred and then after your arrest,

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:19.560
how much did your opinion change of the FBI?

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:24.400
In retrospect, do you think that you were naive in the past to be so supportive of the

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:25.400
FBI?

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:26.400
Oh, yeah.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:27.400
Oh, sure.

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:31.400
My position, my attitude toward the FBI changed dramatically.

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:36.640
You know, frankly, when I was working with the FBI, I just always thought they were

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:37.640
idiots.

00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:41.720
And the CIA and the FBI have had this rivalry going back to the creation of the

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:43.320
CIA in the late 1940s.

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:47.560
And we just always thought they were idiots because we were working together overseas

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:53.080
and the FBI is trying to build a case against John Smith.

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:54.280
We don't care about any case.

00:21:54.280 --> 00:21:56.520
We're just trying to disrupt the next terrorist attack.

00:21:56.520 --> 00:21:59.240
And I always thought, well, they don't prosecute these cases anyway.

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:00.240
This is a waste of time.

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:02.600
I don't even know why we're cooperating with the FBI.

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:05.600
But we humored each other and we were friendly and we would have dinner together and

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:08.520
go out together and do joint operations together.

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:12.200
And now, I believe that the FBI is a malignant force.

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:17.840
I think that it is anathema to the American way of life.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:20.600
It's a domestic spying agency.

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:25.640
They prosecute cases as if we have a National Secrets Act, which we do not.

00:22:25.640 --> 00:22:32.000
They are very happy and willing and quick to use the Espionage Act to target anybody

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:35.320
who is a dissenter in any way.

00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:41.360
You know, all of us who don't toe the party line or disagree with this policy

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:48.880
of constant perpetual war that we have, you're in danger of an espionage investigation.

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:53.440
You write that, I've come to the conclusion over many years of working with, working against

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:59.400
and watching the FBI that the only way they make their cases is to entrap people.

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:04.580
Remember they can only get promoted if they make arrests and the arrests stick.

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:08.400
If that means entrapment or trumped up charges, so be it.

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:12.720
How much do you think the FBI depends on entrapment?

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:17.160
Because there are many critics of their role in the war on terror and the alleged entrapment

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:22.160
of terrorist suspects or terrorist acts that they either never would have conceived, attempted

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:26.240
or been even remotely capable of doing without the FBI.

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:30.800
So how much do you think the FBI actually depends on entrapment?

00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:34.760
I think the FBI depends almost solely on entrapment.

00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:35.760
Sure.

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:40.920
We're going to have the occasional cab driver who's trying to get in touch with ISIS or

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:43.520
trying to make his way to Aleppo or whatever.

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:46.920
You know, you're always going to have idiots doing things like that.

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:49.520
But those aren't the cases we're talking about here.

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:53.600
We're talking about cases like the Cleveland Four, for example.

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:59.680
There were four idiots in Cleveland sitting in a bar when another guy came in who

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:04.360
they knew, although they didn't know he was secretly an FBI informant.

00:24:04.360 --> 00:24:07.120
And this FBI informant said, hey, you know what we should do?

00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:08.280
We should stick it to the man.

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:10.560
We should blow up the Route 82 bridge.

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:14.920
And these half-drunk idiots said, yeah, yeah, we should blow up the Route 82 bridge.

00:24:14.920 --> 00:24:18.600
So the FBI informant provides the explosives, which were inert.

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:23.280
He provides the detonator, and they all go and put the explosives on the Route

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:26.760
82 bridge outside of Cleveland, and it doesn't go off.

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:28.800
And the FBI arrests them.

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:33.720
And they're sentenced to 20, 20, 25, and 30 years in prison.

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:35.080
This wasn't their idea.

00:24:35.080 --> 00:24:37.120
This was the FBI's idea.

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:40.880
These guys would have never had anything to do with the Route 82 bridge.

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:45.080
If the FBI hadn't tasked its informant to go into that bar and

00:24:45.080 --> 00:24:47.320
to recruit them into this operation.

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:50.000
But that's how the FBI works.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:51.080
That's how it operates.

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:53.800
That's how FBI agents get promoted.

00:24:53.800 --> 00:24:57.160
So can the FBI be trusted in your opinion?

00:24:57.160 --> 00:25:00.440
And if not, what does that say for law enforcement and

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:03.080
criminal justice in the United States?

00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:06.360
I really have come to the conclusion that the FBI cannot be trusted.

00:25:06.360 --> 00:25:12.840
Not for any reason, which is a shame because terrorism is real.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:15.200
And terrorism is a threat.

00:25:15.200 --> 00:25:21.080
But I have advised people and I've gone down on record in writing

00:25:21.080 --> 00:25:25.840
as saying that I don't think any American should ever speak to the FBI for any reason.

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:28.880
We are speaking with John Carriacau.

00:25:28.880 --> 00:25:35.960
He is co-chair with past guest Tom Drake and of the Whistleblowers Public Education Campaign.

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:40.480
You can find out more about that by going to John's website, JohnCarriacau.com.

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:44.760
That's K-I-R-I-A-K-O-U.

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:47.520
Back in September when you won the Sam Adams Award, you said,

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:51.200
our country is in crisis, whether it is because of our apparently seamless

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:55.520
escalation into a permanent wartime economy, our inability to wage peace

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:59.640
in the Middle East and South Asia, or our national compulsion to prosecute

00:25:59.640 --> 00:26:02.000
and humiliate national security whistleblowers.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:05.960
The quest for peace must be a part of our presidential election.

00:26:05.960 --> 00:26:09.280
Instead of arguing which candidate would be more likely to use drones,

00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:13.200
more likely to bomb our enemies, reel or perceived, or more likely to use

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:17.240
the stick rather than the carrot, we must demand that those candidates

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:21.920
commit themselves to the pursuit of peace both here and abroad without peace.

00:26:21.920 --> 00:26:26.600
We will continue down the long road toward anarchy and hatred.

00:26:26.600 --> 00:26:28.600
So let's discuss the run for the White House.

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:33.840
How much do you think this 2016 presidential campaign reflects

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:36.480
our current permanent wartime footing?

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:40.520
Do you think the campaign on both the Republican and Democratic side

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:44.480
reflects what you see as a long road toward anarchy and hatred?

00:26:44.480 --> 00:26:46.000
Oh, I do.

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:49.560
Um, boy, where do we start on this?

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:51.600
Right, right.

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:58.320
You've got an unhinged lunatic on the one side and a neocon criminal

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:03.920
on the other side who effectively stole the nomination from Bernie Sanders.

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:08.120
We now know, thanks to WikiLeaks, it's like there's no chance,

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:09.880
there's no hope for any of us.

00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:17.400
But in fact, I think there is some hope and it may not be obvious yet.

00:27:17.440 --> 00:27:23.760
But Bernie Sanders did very well against the institutional candidate.

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:26.240
So there's some hope there.

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:33.640
And for the first time, really since Ross Perot, which is, you know,

00:27:33.640 --> 00:27:39.960
a while ago now, 20 years, people are taking third parties seriously.

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:41.880
We have to wait and see what the final numbers are going to be,

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:44.600
but I think both the Greens and the Libertarians are going to do

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:47.200
vastly better than they did four years ago.

00:27:47.240 --> 00:27:49.640
And even on the Republican side, you know,

00:27:49.640 --> 00:27:52.400
Rand Paul was taken seriously.

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:57.640
And although he didn't last until New Hampshire, there is a movement

00:27:57.640 --> 00:28:03.880
within the Republican Party that just abhors overseas entanglement.

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:05.760
So I think that that's something to look for.

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:08.800
But if you go one level down, you go a little bit deeper,

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:13.280
you see that thanks to Ed Snowden's revelations about NSA

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:16.480
and NSA's crimes against the American people,

00:28:16.520 --> 00:28:20.920
we've got a group of Republicans on the far right,

00:28:20.920 --> 00:28:23.600
like Justin Amash, for example, or Mike Lee,

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:28.040
and Democrats on the far left who really believe in civil liberties

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:36.600
and have joined together to actually repeal some of NSA's programs

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:40.280
that Ed revealed to us back in 2013.

00:28:40.280 --> 00:28:44.200
So it's a long process, but I think that public opinion

00:28:44.200 --> 00:28:47.000
is very slowly starting to turn.

00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:51.520
But for me personally, I really liked both Jill Stein

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:53.560
and Gary Johnson.

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:56.080
And both, I mean, this was selfish of me, I admit,

00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:59.440
but both have said publicly that if elected president,

00:28:59.440 --> 00:29:03.920
they would pardon me and Ed Snowden and Tom Drake and others.

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:06.680
So I very much liked that.

00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:14.560
And the libertarians are on the ballot in all 50 states.

00:29:14.560 --> 00:29:17.360
When I was in prison, I got a letter from Gary Johnson.

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:19.400
I had been in prison about two weeks.

00:29:19.400 --> 00:29:22.320
Gary Johnson wrote me a letter and he said that he thought

00:29:22.320 --> 00:29:23.600
I had been screwed.

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:26.800
And he was very sorry that I had ended up there.

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:29.560
What I didn't know was that he had also sent letters

00:29:29.560 --> 00:29:31.360
to the attorney general and to the director

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:33.880
of the Bureau of Prisons saying that they should be ashamed

00:29:33.880 --> 00:29:36.200
of themselves and that I should be released to my family

00:29:36.200 --> 00:29:38.800
at the very least for home confinement.

00:29:38.800 --> 00:29:40.280
That was a very generous thing to do.

00:29:40.280 --> 00:29:42.800
And then about a week after I got home from prison

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:46.200
in February of last year, Gary Johnson called me

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:49.680
and asked if I would be on the board of directors

00:29:49.680 --> 00:29:52.200
of the Libertarian Party and if I would travel with him.

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:53.720
So I said that I would.

00:29:53.720 --> 00:29:55.520
I appreciated the support.

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:57.600
And actually, I just got back last night

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:00.040
from the West Coast I've gone to about a dozen

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.720
and states with him over the last month or so.

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:06.960
You were among a group of US intelligence veterans, the veteran intelligence professionals

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:12.760
for sanity who signed a letter to President Obama back in May urging an expedited report

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:14.480
on Hillary Clinton's emails.

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:18.920
Past guests on our show who also signed the letter along with you include former NSA technical

00:30:18.920 --> 00:30:24.120
director William Binney, former NSA senior executive Tom Drake, former Foreign Service

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:28.860
officer Matthew Ho, former CIA analyst Ray McGovern, former deputy national intelligence

00:30:28.860 --> 00:30:33.660
officer Elizabeth Murray, former UN weapons inspector Scott Ritter, former Foreign Service

00:30:33.660 --> 00:30:40.780
officer Peter VanBuren, former NSA senior analyst Kirk Wiebe, and former US diplomat Ann Wright.

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:46.720
In that letter it says, what is already known about NSA's collected all electronic practices

00:30:46.720 --> 00:30:52.000
over the past several years strongly suggests that NSA and perhaps the FBI already know

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:56.140
chapter and verse what is in Secretary Clinton's emails.

00:30:56.140 --> 00:31:00.060
It is virtually certain they know what was in Secretary Clinton's emails including the

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:02.380
ones she thought she had deleted.

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:08.380
It is likely that they have also been able to determine which foreign intelligence agencies

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:11.180
and other hackers were able to access the emails.

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:13.540
What ignores this at one's peril?

00:31:13.540 --> 00:31:18.420
Secretary Clinton's security violations can have impact not only on whether she becomes

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:23.380
your successor but also on whether she would in that case be beholden to those

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:28.100
who know what lies hidden from the rest of us, perhaps even from you.

00:31:28.100 --> 00:31:32.860
Do you believe that the NSA and FBI could do to Hillary Clinton something that you have

00:31:32.860 --> 00:31:37.100
suggested that they are doing to all US citizens and that is building a case against her in

00:31:37.100 --> 00:31:38.900
case she gets out of line?

00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:41.580
How vulnerable do you believe she is to blackmail?

00:31:41.580 --> 00:31:45.260
Oh yes, I think that's exactly what could happen.

00:31:45.260 --> 00:31:50.140
Listen, even though J. Edgar Hoover's dead, the spirit of J. Edgar Hoover lives

00:31:50.140 --> 00:31:54.740
on and I think that that's exactly what we're seeing.

00:31:54.740 --> 00:32:01.740
When NSA has the ability to save the telephone metadata, the text messages and the emails

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:10.700
of every American man, woman, and child for the next 500 years, that's just too much

00:32:12.100 --> 00:32:13.180
power.

00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:17.580
It's too much authority and of course they can do with it what they want.

00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:23.140
They have in the past with impunity what's to keep them from doing it in the future.

00:32:23.140 --> 00:32:30.140
When the vested interest really is the FBI and NSA and CIA and their associated agencies,

00:32:31.180 --> 00:32:36.900
presidents come and go but these organizations live on forever and their primary goal is

00:32:36.900 --> 00:32:39.100
to protect themselves.

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:44.900
So yeah, I see there's a bad moon rising to quote Creighton's Clearwater Revival.

00:32:44.900 --> 00:32:48.740
What would you say to somebody who says that, hey, I don't have anything to worry about,

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:55.500
I'm not concerned about NSA or FBI surveillance of me because I don't commit any crimes and

00:32:55.500 --> 00:32:58.500
so why should I be concerned about this?

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:03.240
Why would you suggest that all citizens should be concerned about the kind of surveillance

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:07.420
and the kind of, as you say, the way in which they could build a case against

00:33:07.420 --> 00:33:11.260
people by using NSA and FBI surveillance?

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:16.020
Tom Drake likes to point out in his speeches that the first person to pose that question

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:22.020
publicly, why should I worry because I haven't done anything, was Joseph Gerbal in 1936.

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:29.020
But I would say beyond that, we should look at Dr. Harvey Silverglate, a professor of

00:33:29.020 --> 00:33:34.460
law at Harvard University who wrote a book in 2012 called Three Felonies a Day and he

00:33:34.460 --> 00:33:40.540
argues that we are so over-legislated, so over-regulated and over-criminalized

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:45.860
in our country that the average American on the average day going about his or her

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:49.300
normal business commits three felonies.

00:33:49.300 --> 00:33:53.920
The point being if the government doesn't like your politics or doesn't like your

00:33:53.920 --> 00:34:00.100
face or doesn't like something about you, they can come up with what appear to be

00:34:00.100 --> 00:34:03.980
legitimate charges and they can ruin your life.

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:07.740
They can silence you, they can imprison you, they can ruin the lives of your children

00:34:07.740 --> 00:34:12.620
and your spouse, because that's just the way the system is now.

00:34:12.620 --> 00:34:15.060
So yeah, I think we should all be afraid.

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:19.860
All Americans need to be vigilant against our own government.

00:34:19.860 --> 00:34:23.340
There is an issue where you find all the politicians getting it wrong.

00:34:23.340 --> 00:34:27.780
In mid-October, you wrote an article titled Why Do None of Our Politicians Understand

00:34:27.780 --> 00:34:28.780
Syria?

00:34:28.780 --> 00:34:32.380
In it, you write, one major complaint I had about the George W. Bush administration

00:34:32.380 --> 00:34:36.540
when I was a CIA officer was that I had never seen a president work so hard to

00:34:36.540 --> 00:34:38.660
not talk to other countries.

00:34:38.660 --> 00:34:41.900
Don't like Iraq, don't talk to Saddam Hussein just invade.

00:34:41.900 --> 00:34:45.380
Don't like the political positions of Western European countries, insult them as old

00:34:45.380 --> 00:34:48.460
Europe and court Eastern European countries in their place.

00:34:48.460 --> 00:34:52.020
When Washington has poor relations with other countries, that makes the CIA's job of

00:34:52.020 --> 00:34:55.420
collecting intelligence to inform policy harder.

00:34:55.420 --> 00:34:59.060
That failure to collect intelligence leads to poor analysis, which leads to poor

00:34:59.060 --> 00:35:02.660
policy decisions at the State Department and the White House.

00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:06.820
But don't poor relations win votes?

00:35:06.820 --> 00:35:09.420
Isn't negotiating with the enemy a loser at election time?

00:35:09.420 --> 00:35:12.020
And how much do vote-winning policies?

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:15.740
How much does politics undermine good policy decisions?

00:35:15.740 --> 00:35:23.820
Well, you know, I would say that in a perfect world, good diplomacy ought to win elections.

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:29.300
I mean, it was Nixon who went to China, right, in 72.

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:34.860
It was Nixon who ordered negotiations with the North Vietnamese that essentially ended

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:41.740
any chance that the Democrats had to have foreign policy as a campaign issue.

00:35:41.740 --> 00:35:48.940
Our problem is that this neo-conservative, or in the case of the Democrats,

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:54.940
neoliberal, militarist foreign policy has become the norm.

00:35:54.940 --> 00:36:00.860
And so even people like John Kerry—I worked very closely with John Kerry for two and a half

00:36:00.860 --> 00:36:01.860
years.

00:36:01.860 --> 00:36:06.780
I got a receipt into John Kerry's thinking on foreign policy, and I remember coming home

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:12.140
and telling my wife one day, the American people have no idea how conservative John

00:36:12.140 --> 00:36:13.140
Kerry is.

00:36:13.140 --> 00:36:14.860
He's no liberal.

00:36:14.860 --> 00:36:17.780
I saw it with my own eyes.

00:36:17.780 --> 00:36:21.660
Another problem with Kerry and with Clinton and with others of their ilk is that they

00:36:21.660 --> 00:36:26.900
always think that they are the smartest people in the room, and so they don't take

00:36:26.900 --> 00:36:29.100
advice from any of their advisors.

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:34.900
Well, why have advisors if you're so smart that you know exactly the right thing to do?

00:36:34.900 --> 00:36:36.380
And then they act on their own.

00:36:36.380 --> 00:36:39.380
As a result, we have war in Syria.

00:36:39.380 --> 00:36:40.900
We have war in Iraq.

00:36:40.900 --> 00:36:42.660
We have war in Libya.

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:44.420
We have chaos in Egypt.

00:36:44.420 --> 00:36:47.300
We have a never-ending war in Afghanistan and Pakistan.

00:36:47.300 --> 00:36:49.660
We're involved in a civil war in Yemen.

00:36:49.660 --> 00:36:52.660
And somehow we're supposed to think that this is what the State Department is supposed

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:53.660
to do.

00:36:53.660 --> 00:36:55.820
It's just simply wrong.

00:36:55.820 --> 00:37:00.500
When you write about the Bush administration's shortcomings when it comes to foreign policy,

00:37:00.500 --> 00:37:04.700
you're right that I thought that would change when Barack Obama was elected president.

00:37:04.700 --> 00:37:08.060
After all, he had already won the Nobel Peace Prize, but under the leadership of Hillary

00:37:08.060 --> 00:37:12.060
Clinton and John Kerry, the State Department and American diplomatic policy have proven

00:37:12.060 --> 00:37:16.260
to be little more than extensions of what we had under George W. Bush.

00:37:16.260 --> 00:37:19.940
This is most clearly exemplified in U.S. policy towards Syria.

00:37:19.940 --> 00:37:25.380
Now, I know that you were talking about Jill Stein and Gary Johnson earlier, but how different

00:37:25.380 --> 00:37:30.460
would that situation be under a President Donald Trump?

00:37:30.460 --> 00:37:35.580
Oh, you just sent chills up my spine.

00:37:35.580 --> 00:37:40.140
Yeah, I think it would be, wow, I can't even believe I'm going to say this.

00:37:40.140 --> 00:37:44.620
I think it would be even worse, as if it could get any worse.

00:37:44.780 --> 00:37:50.940
What was it that Trump said, we need to bomb the crap out of him recently?

00:37:50.940 --> 00:37:53.780
He knows more about ISIS than the generals know.

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:57.740
Yeah, I mean, look at Trump's foreign policy team.

00:37:57.740 --> 00:38:02.740
It's like besides John Bolton, who's an arch neocon, who are these people?

00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:05.980
I mean, I've been in Washington since 1982.

00:38:05.980 --> 00:38:12.300
I spent almost my entire adult life in intelligence, either living in or working on the Middle

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:13.300
East.

00:38:13.300 --> 00:38:17.660
I know a single one of Donald Trump's foreign policy advisors, besides John Bolton, who I

00:38:17.660 --> 00:38:20.140
believe happens to be a war criminal.

00:38:20.140 --> 00:38:25.860
So yeah, I think things would be even worse under Trump.

00:38:25.860 --> 00:38:30.180
But things are going to be bad enough if Clinton wins, but even worse under Trump.

00:38:30.180 --> 00:38:34.580
Right, because you've been very critical of the potential appointment of Michael

00:38:34.580 --> 00:38:38.340
Morrell, who you used to work with in the CIA.

00:38:38.340 --> 00:38:42.700
What do you think that Michael Morrell, if he became the CIA director?

00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:47.300
If Hillary Clinton won the election, and one of her first announcements of her cabinet

00:38:47.300 --> 00:38:52.580
members was Michael Morrell as her CIA director, what would that tell us about the upcoming

00:38:52.580 --> 00:38:55.060
four years of Hillary Clinton as president?

00:38:55.060 --> 00:39:00.580
I think that that would be the clearest possible indication that absolutely nothing

00:39:00.580 --> 00:39:06.020
will change from the George W. Bush and Barack Obama intelligence policy.

00:39:06.020 --> 00:39:10.540
Mike Morrell was the executive director of the CIA, the number three ranking officer

00:39:10.540 --> 00:39:13.540
of the CIA under George W. Bush.

00:39:13.540 --> 00:39:18.060
Under Obama, he was the deputy director for intelligence, and then he was the deputy director

00:39:18.060 --> 00:39:20.060
of the whole CIA.

00:39:20.060 --> 00:39:25.660
This is something that many Americans forget, that at the sub-cabinet level, personnel

00:39:25.660 --> 00:39:27.620
really don't change over.

00:39:27.620 --> 00:39:32.260
So you have these people who came of age in senior leadership positions under George

00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:38.060
W. Bush, and then were promoted by Barack Obama.

00:39:38.060 --> 00:39:42.620
So now they've risen to the point where they're ready to join the cabinet under a president

00:39:42.620 --> 00:39:43.620
Hillary Clinton.

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:50.380
I think this is an extraordinarily dangerous development, a very dangerous policy, and

00:39:50.380 --> 00:39:52.740
something that really needs to be looked at.

00:39:52.740 --> 00:39:56.420
But then that leads to another problem, and that's the problem of the utter lack

00:39:56.420 --> 00:39:58.420
of congressional oversight.

00:39:58.420 --> 00:39:59.940
You know, I say this all the time in speeches.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:07.000
Frank Church is dead. Otis Pike is dead. Ted Kennedy is dead. There's no real congressional oversight anymore.

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:13.000
You have the Senate Select Committee on Intelligence and the House Permanent Select Committee on Intelligence

00:40:13.000 --> 00:40:19.000
that are supposed to be in charge of making sure that the CIA operates within the confines of the law.

00:40:19.000 --> 00:40:26.000
But instead what you have is those committees acting solely as cheerleaders for the CIA

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:30.000
and literally letting them get away with murder.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:38.000
One of your criticisms as is many people's criticism of the Bush administration is that the rock war was politicized.

00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:44.000
So how much has war been depoliticized since the end of the Bush administration?

00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:52.000
And can it really be depoliticized? Is this something that more reflects a bigger cultural issue within intelligence?

00:40:52.000 --> 00:41:00.000
I do. I think that there's been this transition among the American populace where we just have come to expect war.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:07.000
It's just a normal thing for us to bomb other countries, to invade other countries, to send special forces into other countries.

00:41:07.000 --> 00:41:16.000
It's the norm now and it was never the norm even under presidents like Ronald Reagan or George H.W. Bush.

00:41:16.000 --> 00:41:19.000
So why does war win votes?

00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:32.000
You know, it makes us seem like we're tough. Americans tend to be jingoistic and these chants of USA, USA ring true for a lot of people.

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:35.000
I think people like being the big boy on the block.

00:41:35.000 --> 00:41:42.000
And when they're not sending their own kids into battle to be killed for no apparent reason, I think it's an easy thing to do.

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:50.000
We have been speaking with John Carriaco. He is co-chair with past guest Tom Drake of the Whistleblowers Public Education Campaign.

00:41:50.000 --> 00:42:01.000
You can find out more about that campaign by going to John's website, johncarriaco.com, where you can also purchase his book, Reluctant Spy, My Secret Life and the CIA's War on Terror.

00:42:01.000 --> 00:42:07.000
John, for every one of our guests, our final question is always the question from hell, the question we hate to ask.

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:10.000
You might hate to answer our audience is going to hate your response.

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:18.000
Our question from hell for you is, who do you think is more of a danger to the national security of the United States, Hillary Clinton or Donald Trump?

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:21.000
Oh boy. And I have to pick one?

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:24.000
Yes, that's why it's the question from hell.

00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:28.000
Oh jeez.

00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:36.000
Hillary Clinton. And I say that because I think Donald Trump is more likely to be impeached quickly.

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:39.000
And removed from office.

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:43.000
So are you just basing this on which one will be removed from office quicker?

00:42:43.000 --> 00:42:46.000
More quickly.

00:42:46.000 --> 00:42:48.000
John.

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:52.000
John, it's really a pleasure having you on the show. Thank you so much.

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:54.000
And I'm going to be bugging you in the future to have you back on.

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:56.000
I'm looking forward to that. Thanks for having me.

00:42:56.000 --> 00:43:00.000
All right, take care.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:04.000
You've been listening to a This Is Hell interview.

00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:08.000
For more hell, visit ThisIsHell.com.

